<h1 class = "catalog-desc-header">Save File Location</h1>

<div style = "display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 15vh;"> 
    <div style = "flex: 2;">
        <section class = "catalog-desc-container">
            <h2 class = "catalog-desc-subheader">On Windows: </h3>
            <code class = "catalog-desc-file-location">
                C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\Celeste\Saves
            </code>
        </section>

        <section class = "catalog-desc-container">
            <h2 class = "catalog-desc-subheader">On macOS: </h3>
            <code class = "catalog-desc-file-location">
                /Users/[username]/Library/Application Support/Celeste/Saves
            </code>
        </section>

        <section class = "catalog-desc-container">
            <h2 class = "catalog-desc-subheader">On Linux: </h3>
            <code class = "catalog-desc-file-location">
                /home/[username]/.local/share/Celeste/Saves
            </code>
        </section>

        <p class = "catalog-desc-text"> 
            After locating the save folder, there <rem>possibly</rem> 3 primary save files.
            Each one corresponds to the 3 save files that you have on Celeste.
        </p>
        <ul class = "catalog-desc-text">
            <li><code class = "catalog-desc-text">0.celeste: </code> Save File #1</li>
            <li><code class = "catalog-desc-text">1.celeste: </code> Save File #2</li>
            <li><code class = "catalog-desc-text">2.celeste: </code> Save File #3</li>
        </ul>
        <p class = "catalog-desc-text"> 
            Drag your desired save file in our editor to edit.
        </p>

    </div>

    <section style = "flex: 1; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;">
        <img class = "catalog-desc-img" src = "/Photos/Celeste-save-file-structure.png" style = "flex: 1">
    </section>
</div>

<h1 class = "catalog-desc-header">Encoding Algorithm Summary</h1>
<p class = "catalog-desc-text">
    Celeste doesn't encode their text but uses <strong>XML</strong> to structure their code.
    Because of this, decoding to JSON is a little more difficult. <br><br>There are 2 primary sections,
    the <strong>global</strong> variables, and the <strong>area specific</strong> variables. On the XML,
    it would look like:
</p>

<code class = "catalog-desc-text" style = "white-space: pre-wrap; overflow-x: auto;">
&lt;SaveData&gt;
    &lt;!-- GLOBAL VARIABLES SECTION --&gt;
    &lt;Version&gt;1.4.0.0&lt;/Version&gt;
    &lt;Name&gt;Madeline&lt;/Name&gt;
    ...etc
    &lt;RevealedChapter9&gt;true&lt;/RevealedChapter9&gt;
    &lt;LastArea ID="1" Mode="Normal"&gt;&lt;/LastArea&gt;
    
    &lt;!-- AREA SPECIFIC SECTION --&gt;
    &lt;Areas&gt;
        ...Area stuff
    &lt;/Areas&gt;
&lt;/SaveData&gt;<br>
</code>

<p class = "catalog-desc-text">
    Our decoder <strong>ONLY</strong> reads the global sections since they don't contain
    much nested XML formatting, and don't contain attributes.
</p>

<p class = "catalog-desc-text">
    Lastly, the contents of the XML file are very easy to change. Opening the file
    on a text editor can still easily allow you to edit the variables. Escpecially
    the <code class = "catalog-desc-text"><strong>&lt;Areas&gt;</strong></code> section.
</p>

<p class = "catalog-desc-text">
    To understand more about Celeste save files, visit <a href="https://frissyn.gitbook.io/celeste-saves">this website</a> to see more.
</p>

<p class = "catalog-desc-text" >
    Here is the summary of how we decoded it:
    <ol class = "catalog-desc-text" style = "margin-bottom: 15vh;">
        <li>Take .sav raw save file</li>
        <li>Use an XMLParser and XMLBuilder</li>
        <li>Create a JSON with only neccessary variables (Global values)</li>
        <li>Then display that to the user</li>
    </ol>
</p>
        